{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/architecture/ui-v2/screens/reviews-administration.md docs/architecture/ui-v2/screens/administration-inventory.md'","aggregated_output":"# Reviews, Administration and key rotation\n\nSource: `R2App` (`REVIEWS` line 826, `rvKind`, `canApply`, `doApply`,\n`appliedKey`, `appliedOther`, `TOPICS`, `PROFILES`, `verdictFor`, `T` admin\ntables, API keys row actions). Chunk: C31. Every change goes through the\nexisting Configuration review (`changes.rs`, D014): the review binds tenant,\ncredentials, resource and draft, and changed context invalidates it.\n\n## Administration\n\n- Accordion of the products in DESIGN-V2 §6. Each admitted product expands to\n  tabs from `AdminInventory`, generated from the complete table in\n  [administration-inventory.md](administration-inventory.md): every tab is\n  mapped to an admitted read and every creation or row action to an admitted\n  `ChangeKind` + `ChangeAction` with its label, and every denied action,\n  unavailable tab and removed tab is named there. A product or tab with no\n  admitted service renders as **Not available in this release** with the\n  dependency it waits on; it never shows invented inventory and never offers\n  a mutation (V2-T20, V2-R11).\n- Tables: columns per tab, row actions \"Edit…\", \"Rotate…\", \"Finish\n  rotation…\", \"View\", \"Results\". Read-only products (Red Team, Model/skill)\n  never offer a change.\n- Security profiles and Custom topics derive \"Used by\" from one topic\n  inventory; detector ids and topic keys are distinct types.\n\n## Review (I3)\n\n- Opened by `openReview{kind, action, resource}`; the scope carries tenant,\n  resource, before/after diff lines, impact (from `changes::impact`), and the\n  typed-name requirement.\n- The UI shows the diff, impact, a text field \"Type <name> to confirm\" and\n  Apply (disabled until match). `confirmReview{review_id, typed}` is\n  validated in the core: wrong name, different tenant or changed context\n  refuses with the reason, and the UI shows a warn toast naming the tenant.\n- Applied panel (non-key): \"Live. The item was read back.\" with name,\n  revision, time, tenant. Done. Unknown outcome renders the existing\n  \"not confirmed\" state with the reconciliation step, never a success.\n- Any review exit (Done, Escape, tenant switch, context retirement) calls\n  `ForgetSecret` for any held one-time key and resets `secretVisible` (I6).\n\n## Key rotation (I6, I7) — supported sequence\n\nThe prototype promised a grace period with a later revocation and a secret\nrecoverable from the Keychain. The retained core supports neither as one\noperation: `ChangeAction::Rotate` regenerates a key in place (no grace), and\n`ChangeAction::Version` creates an additional key while the existing one is\nunchanged (`changes.rs` 842–846, `management.rs` 980–988). Issued keys are\nheld per result for the active context only and never persisted\n(DESIGN.md \"Secrets\"). The implemented flow therefore uses the two admitted\noperations in sequence and never stores a secret. It applies to Runtime\nSecurity API keys only: Gateway service and user keys admit `Rotate`\n(regenerate in place) and not `Version`, so they get the plain \"Rotate…\"\nreview with the existing one-time reveal and no staged sequence\n(administration-inventory.md):\n\n1. **Issue an additional key** — review `ApiKeys · Version` for the\n   application. On apply the core reads the key back, holds the one-time\n   secret in the zeroizing buffer, and the panel reads **Additional key\n   issued · old key still active**: Reveal (host `RevealSecret` sheet, 60 s,\n   selectable label) and Copy (host clipboard with marker, cleared after\n   60 s); note \"Shown once. Not stored by this app; if lost, issue another\n   key\"; actions **Finish later** / **Application updated**. Side effects:\n   Inbox item `rotation-<application>` (Needs action, \"Update\n   support-copilot with the new key, then remove the old key\"), with a\n   snooze that returns in 24 hours and no invented deadline; the key table\n   shows both keys, the old one marked \"Replace after deployment\".\n2. **Resumed** — panel reads **Rotation in progress · secret no longer\n   available**: \"The new key was shown once when it was issued and this app\n   does not keep it. If support-copilot does not have it, issue another key.\"\n   Actions: **Issue another key** (returns to step 1) / **Application\n   updated**.\n3. **Application updated** — records a *local* deployment acknowledgement\n   with its time (\"Deployment acknowledged 07:51 by you on this Mac\"; this is\n   the operator's statement, not remote evidence) and opens the review\n   `ApiKeys · Delete` for the old key.\n4. **Old key removed** — on apply the core performs the delete and reads\n   back; absent is the expected readback and the panel reads **Rotation\n   complete · old key removed 07:52** with the readback time stored once and\n   never recomputed. The Inbox item clears and the completion toast fires.\n   An unknown delete outcome shows the reconciliation state and keeps the\n   Inbox item.\n\nTwo timestamps are kept and labelled distinctly: `deployment_acknowledged_at`\n(local statement) and `old_key_removed_at` (remote readback). Neither is\nderived from read counts. `ChangeAction::Rotate` (regenerate in place) stays\navailable from the key row as \"Regenerate…\" with the existing impact text\n\"Replaces this key; update every integration using it\"; it is not called\nrotation in the UI.\n\n## Playground verdicts\n\n- Detector results and topic matches are independent. A profile without the\n  detector, or without the topic assigned, yields \"Not detected\" with the\n  reason; monitor-only turns Blocked into Flagged; applying a topic never\n  suppresses an enabled detector. The applied topic's example prompts\n  classify under the topic and show Flagged when assigned.\n\n## Fixtures and tests\n\n- Boards R2Admin, R2Review. Tests V2-R03 (confirmReview refusals), V2-R06\n  (held key dropped on every review exit, tenant switch and context\n  retirement; Reveal after drop refuses), V2-R07 (rotation state machine:\n  Version → acknowledge → Delete → readback, with these named failure\n  cases: (a) failed issuance: refusal shown, no key held, no Inbox item;\n  (b) unknown issuance (uncertain Version outcome): state \"issuance not\n  confirmed\" with the existing reconciliation read of the application's key\n  inventory, no automatic reissue, no Inbox item until a key is read back;\n  (c) rejected or failed Delete: the reminder stays, the old key stays listed,\n  `old_key_removed_at` stays unset and the panel stays at stage 3 with the\n  refusal reason; (d) unknown Delete outcome: \"removal not confirmed\" with\n  reconciliation by readback, reminder retained; (e) both timestamps stable\n  across reads and tenant switches), V2-T03, T06 (resumed panel has no secret\n  controls), T07 (Inbox item lifecycle), T08 (timestamps labelled and\n  stable), V2-T19 (unavailable product renders no inventory and no action).\n- Live (C31 only): one topic review apply and read-back against the\n  change-allowed tenant under the `airs-v2-c25-` prefix, with cleanup. Key\n  issuance and deletion are not run live in P09; they are covered by the\n  C09 fixtures and the existing C25 L-rows.\n# Administration inventory: every prototype product, tab and action\n\nSource: `prototype/R2App.dc.html` `PRODUCTS` (tabs, `single`, `readOnly`),\nthe `T` tables (`rt:Security profiles`, `rt:Custom topics`, `rt:API keys`,\n`gw:Routes`, `rtm:Scans`, `sc:Skill scans`, `ais:Tool policies`,\n`aes:Discovered AI`, `aes:Policies`) and row actions (line 1099). Admitted\noperations are the retained core's: `ManagementResource` reads and\n`changes::allowed(kind, action)` (`changes.rs` 253–268), `GatewayRead` and\n`gateway_changes::allowed` (`gateway_changes.rs`), and the C13 read-only\nobservation functions in `red_team.rs`. The `AdminInventory` scope (C31) is\ngenerated from this table and tested against it (V2-T20); the core refuses\nany (kind, action) outside the matrices (existing `allowed`, V2-R11).\n\nDispositions: **Read** (admitted read, rows shown), **Change** (admitted\nreviewed change with the exact actions), **Unavailable** (no admitted\nservice: tab renders \"Not available in this release\" with the gate, no rows,\nno actions), **Removed** (prototype tab not carried, with reason).\n\n## AI Runtime Security (`rt`)\n\nReads: `ManagementResource::{Profiles, Topics, Applications, ApiKeys,\nDeploymentProfiles, DlpCatalog, DataProfiles, DataPatterns,\nFilteringProfiles, Dictionaries, Sessions}`.\n\n| Tab | Disposition | Reads | Admitted actions (`ChangeKind` · `ChangeAction`) | Denied in the UI (never offered) | Fixture / contract |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Overview | Read | counts from the reads above; `ScanRecordView` | — | — | C08/C12 fixtures |\n| Playground | Read + scan | `scan.rs` (existing synchronous scan) | — | — | C03 scan fixtures |\n| Security profiles | Change | Profiles | Profiles · Create (\"New profile…\"), Update (\"Edit…\"), Delete | — | C09 fixtures |\n| Custom topics | Change | Topics | Topics · Create (\"New topic…\"), Update (\"Edit…\"), Delete | — | C09 fixtures |\n| Applications | Change | Applications | Applications · Update (\"Edit…\"), Delete | **Create** (not admitted; no \"New application…\") | C09 |\n| API keys | Change | ApiKeys | ApiKeys · Create (\"New API key…\"), Version (\"Issue additional key…\"), Rotate (\"Regenerate…\"), Delete (\"Remove…\") | **Update** (not admitted; no \"Edit…\") | C09 one-time secrets |\n| DLP | Change | DlpCatalog, DataPatterns, DataProfiles, FilteringProfiles, Dictionaries | DataPatterns · Create/Update/Delete; Dictionaries · Create/Update/Delete; DataProfiles · Create/Update; FilteringProfiles · Update | **DataProfiles · Delete**, **FilteringProfiles · Create/Delete**, **DlpCatalog · any** | C10 fixtures |\n| Deployment profiles | Read | DeploymentProfiles | — | any change | C08 |\n\n## AI Gateway (`gw`)\n\nReads: `GatewayRead::{Inventory, Detail, IntegrationModels, Prerequisites,\nObserve, McpServers}`.\n\n| Tab | Disposition | Reads | Admitted actions | Denied in the UI | Fixture / contract |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Overview | Read | Observe, Prerequisites | — | — | C16/C18 |\n| Workspaces | Change | Inventory/Detail (workspaces) | GatewayWorkspaces · Create (\"New workspace…\"), Update, Delete | Rotate | C16/C17 |\n| Routes | Change | Inventory/Detail (routes) | GatewayRoutes · Create (\"New route…\"), Update, Delete | Version | C17 |\n| Providers | Change | Inventory/Detail (providers) | GatewayProviders · Create (\"New provider…\"), Update, Delete | Models | C17 |\n| Guardrails | Change | Inventory/Detail (guardrails) | GatewayGuardrails · Create (\"New guardrail…\"), Update, Delete | — | C17 |\n| Keys | Change | Inventory (service keys, user keys) | GatewayServiceKeys / GatewayUserKeys · Create (\"New key…\"), Update (\"Edit…\"), Rotate (\"Rotate…\", regenerates in place: `GatewayApiKeyRotateRequestSchema`, one-time reveal through the host, no staged sequence), Delete (\"Remove…\") | **Version** (not admitted: no \"Issue additional key…\", no \"Finish rotation…\", no rotation reminder) | C17 |\n| Integrations (prototype folds into Providers) | Change | IntegrationModels | GatewayIntegrations · Create, Update, Delete, Models (\"Replace model list…\") | Rotate | C17 |\n| Limits | **Removed** | — | — | — | the prototype's route budget limits have no admitted read or change; budgets appear in Routes detail only when the service returns them (C16) |\n| Logs | Read | Observe (telemetry) | — | any creation (no \"New…\" on an activity tab) | C18 |\n| MCP servers (in AIS in the prototype) | Read | McpServers | — | — | C22; used by Harness lifecycle 2 (P16) |\n\n## AI Red Teaming (`rtm`, read-only, `readOnly: true`)\n\nReads: `red_team.rs` — `statistics`, `red_team_scans`, `attacks` (paged).\n\n| Tab | Disposition | Reads | Actions | Fixture |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Overview | Read | statistics | none | C13 |\n| Targets | Read (identifiers and types only) | `target_id` and `target_type` as carried by `red_team_scans` (`red_team.rs` 56–66); target names are not admitted by the C13 privacy boundary and are never shown | none; \"Results\" opens the scan | C13; deviation P22; V2-T21 asserts no target name appears in the rendered tab or in any scope the tab reads |\n| Scans | Read | red_team_scans | none | C13 |\n| Attack results | Read | attacks | none | C13 |\n| Reports | Read | static reports (existing C13 metadata-only) | none; no execution | C13 |\n\nNew scans, re-runs and \"hand the spec to a scan\" never dispatch anything;\nTest Cases \"Inspect\" applies (P21).\n\n## Model/skill observability (`sc`, \"AI Supply Chain\" in the prototype)\n\nReads: `red_team.rs` — `model_scans`, `skill_scans` (pseudonymised),\n`skill_statistics`.\n\n| Tab | Disposition | Reads | Actions | Fixture |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Overview | Read | skill_statistics, model_scans counts | none | C13 |\n| Model scans | Read | model_scans | none | C13 |\n| Skill scans | Read | skill_scans | none | C13 |\n| Codebase scans | **Unavailable** | none admitted | none | gate: an admitted codebase-scan read |\n| Policies | **Unavailable** (`single: 'policy'` not carried) | none | none; no \"New policy…\" | gate: an admitted supply-chain policy service |\n\nThe product is shown under its C13 name (\"Model and skill scans\"), not as\na scanning product.\n\n## Agentic Identity Security (`ais`) — Unavailable\n\n| Tab | Disposition |\n|---|---|\n| Overview, Tool policies, Identities (Idira), Invocation log | **Unavailable**: \"Not available in this release · needs an admitted AIS service (D058)\". No rows, no \"New policy…\", no review. |\n| MCP servers | carried as a Gateway tab (above) because the read is `GatewayRead::McpServers` |\n\n## Agentic Endpoint Security (`aes`) — Unavailable\n\n| Tab | Disposition |\n|---|---|\n| Overview, Endpoints, Discovered AI, Extensions & packages, Policies | **Unavailable** with the same notice. The prototype's `REVIEWS.aes` policy review, the `aes:Discovered AI` and `aes:Policies` tables and the wizard AES warning demo are fixtures only and are not implemented. |\n\n## Row actions\n\n| Prototype action | Carried as | Condition |\n|---|---|---|\n| Edit… | review `Update` | admitted Update for the tab's kind |\n| Rotate… (Runtime API keys) | review `ApiKeys · Version` labelled \"Issue additional key…\" (the staged rotation sequence of screens/reviews-administration.md) and, separately, `ApiKeys · Rotate` labelled \"Regenerate…\" | Runtime Security API keys only |\n| Rotate… (Gateway keys) | review `GatewayServiceKeys`/`GatewayUserKeys · Rotate` labelled \"Rotate…\": regenerate in place with the existing one-time reveal; no additional-key step, no acknowledgement, no Inbox reminder | Gateway service and user keys only; `Version` is not admitted for Gateway keys |\n| Finish rotation… | opens the Runtime rotation panel (local acknowledgement, then review `ApiKeys · Delete` of the old key) | Runtime rotation in progress only |\n| View | detail sheet (read) | always for Read tabs |\n| Results | detail of a read-only scan | Red Team / Model-skill |\n\n## Tests\n\n- V2-T20: for every (product, tab) above, the rendered tab matches its\n  disposition: Read/Change tabs show rows and exactly the listed actions;\n  Unavailable tabs show the notice and no rows or actions; Removed tabs do\n  not exist.\n- V2-R11: the core refuses every (kind, action) pair in the \"Denied\" column\n  (already enforced by `allowed`; the test pins the matrix so a UI label\n  cannot outrun it), including `GatewayServiceKeys`/`GatewayUserKeys ·\n  Version`.\n- V2-T20 additionally asserts that Gateway key rows render \"Rotate…\" and\n  never \"Issue additional key…\" or \"Finish rotation…\", and that no Gateway\n  rotation creates an Inbox reminder.\n- V2-T21: the Red Team Targets tab renders identifiers and types only; a\n  fixture scan reply carrying a `target_name` field is rendered without it\n  and the field never reaches the `AdminInventory` scope.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/adr/0001-native-operation-authority.md docs/adr/0002-academy-shared-presentation.md docs/adr/0003-native-harness-and-scoped-identity.md docs/adr/0004-managed-workbench-admission.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/adr/0001-native-operation-authority.md docs/adr/0002-academy-shared-presentation.md docs/adr/0003-native-harness-and-scoped-identity.md docs/adr/0004-managed-workbench-admission.md'","aggregated_output":"# 1. Native operation authority owns coordination\n\nDate: 2026-10-16. Status: selected following owner candidate selection; independent G01 verdict\nand implementation readiness are tracked in planning/status.md. Implements D008,D009,D013,D014,D016,D019.\n\nV1 already has deep domain/request modules, but GPUI callers still coordinate\nslots, protected-key IO and ordered effects. React must not inherit that authority.\n\nChoose a principal-bound Rust actor with dispatch(Intent) and observe(ViewScope).\nKeep feature transitions domain-owned inside one initial core crate. Native\nbootstrap injects adapters and creates operator/Harness handles; JSON cannot\nselect the caller principal. Finite domain instructions exclude arbitrary SDK\nmethod strings and any Harness Apply capability.\n\nThe interface includes atomic admission/context retirement, per-source supersession,\nseparate read/review/write/stream domains, priority/deadline/rate-limit semantics,\nbounded queues, bounded delivery-sequence deduplication with safe session rotation,\nsingle-use settlement and truthful Remote outcome. No remote IO\nor blocking vault call occurs while the authoritative transition is locked.\nAdmitted work runs on the host's one async runtime; native adapters execute effects.\n\nReact owns raw editor text/undo. Rust owns immutable captured drafts/reviews.\nReview/Apply/Save/Send capture fresh input in their actual production handlers;\nApply compares fresh input with the stored reviewed operation and executes only\nthat stored operation. Invalid/lossy input, changed context/draft/resource or a\nconsumed review refuses dispatch. Never rely only on a debounced revision.\nRequestApply uses a native operator confirmation; actual Apply completion is\nprivate, single-use and revalidated after the modal editor/context lease.\nEvery scoped intent/observation carries native-issued context and principal\nadmission. Async vault completions are generation/lease-bound; obsolete writes\nreceive orphan cleanup rather than reactivating credentials. Explicit one-time\nsecret reveal/copy stays in a scoped transient native dialog, never snapshots.\n\nAlternatives considered under the design-it-twice skill:\n\n| Design | Depth/locality | Cost |\n|---|---|---|\n| Actor dispatch/observe | Callers learn one authority seam; context and settlement stay together | Finite unions and observation backpressure need discipline |\n| Typed scoped handles | Strong domain methods and revoked scopes; flexible internal composition | More handles/methods and IPC registries for callers to coordinate |\n| Task-oriented scan board | Trivial first scan workflow | Public task surface grows across all parity features |\n\nRecommend the actor with domain-specific types and a small generated presentation\nfacade. Retain the alternatives' explicit scoped validation and effortless scan\nusage internally. Do not combine all their public surfaces or add pass-through\nmodules merely to obtain two method names.\n\nTwo real execution adapters (SDK/scripted), storage adapters (Keychain/session/\nfault-injectable) and clocks justify internal seams. The interface is the test\nsurface: switch/replace/cancel while queued/dispatched, old/duplicate reply,\nfresh-input races, double Apply, denied Harness Apply, unknown outcome/no replay,\npriority/deadline/rate-limit containment and observation resynchronization.\n\nSee [selected design](../architecture/DESIGN.md) for ownership/wire details.\nNo app code has been written and no native/SDK test is claimed by this ADR.\n# 2. Share Academy presentation through explicit composition\n\nDate: 2026-10-16. Status: selected following owner candidate selection; independent G01 verdict\nand implementation readiness are tracked in planning/status.md. Implements D004,D005,D006,D007,D010.\n\nThe current UI package re-exports web app source. Shell palette/mobile navigation\nand theme persistence require tRPC/Clerk/router context; the authenticated web\nentry also starts learner and manager behavior. A whole-app import would spread\nthat authority into unrelated desktop features.\n\nKeep one Academy-owned presentation implementation. Create explicit provider-free\ncontrols/theme/shell exports while preserving the existing tooling entry. Both\nAcademy web and desktop consume the extracted source. Composition adapters supply\nbrand/navigation/account/header/palette/mobile slots; the shared module owns\nvisual semantics, responsive layout and focus. No universal plugin framework.\n\nDesktop global theme is an app preference stored through Rust, not hosted\nsettings.update. Academy web keeps its learner-account theme behavior. Learner\nnotifications, gamification and account effects mount only in Academy composition.\n\nExtract learner views separately when C05/C06 require them. Retain React Query/\ntRPC hooks behind a transport/navigation provider; type-only learner contracts\nbuild without the Express/DB runtime. Web uses existing HTTP authentication;\ndesktop uses a terminating link into the allowlisted Rust Academy transport.\nTokens stay native. Hosted learning/access/grading/award truth and client-safe\nbundle/draft contracts remain authoritative. Never bundle raw answer content.\n\nDirectly embedding the authenticated web root is rejected because its interface\ncontains unrelated account/manager assumptions. Maintaining a desktop UI copy\nis rejected because appearance and learner behavior would diverge.\n\nTests render shared presentation without hosted identity/query providers, run\nboth consumers' regressions, verify native keyboard/focus/themes, and prove no\nlearner mutation/gamification effect from general desktop screens. Learner tests\ncover native link envelopes/allowlist, account cache retirement and hosted authority.\n\nDependency/source/license pins are extraction prerequisites. This ADR changes\nno Academy checkout, package or hosted registration. See\n[selected design](../architecture/DESIGN.md).\n# 3. Retain native Harness and own scoped identity in Rust\n\nDate: 2026-10-16. Status: selected following owner candidate selection; independent G01 verdict\nand implementation readiness are tracked in planning/status.md. Implements D010,D011,D014–D016,D021–D023.\n\nDesktop-server is already a Rust engine. V1 host workers and its package manifest\nstill require Node/managed CLI. Preserve engine depth and replace those host\nrequirements, rather than rewrite the agent. Source inspection alone does not\nestablish that a newly sealed installed bundle works without the old resources.\n\nNative lifecycle owns sealed executable selection, bounded stdin bootstrap,\nprotocol supervision, process cleanup, scoped grants and protected history.\nReact only presents lifecycle state. Grant/tenant/project changes retire the\nrun; approvals bind actual call items. Preserve existing project/process/tool\nrestrictions, Gateway-only inference/MCP and no engine Apply.\n\nIdentity shares native discovery/HTTP/validation/storage mechanics while retaining\nAcademy learner, Harness inference and MCP grant policies. They are distinct\nidentities with distinct sign-out/refresh/context effects. Use public-client\nPKCE/device flows and a no-redirect native transport; never embed a client secret\nor trust renderer-selected issuer/token destinations.\n\nHost signature/JWKS validation is a deliberate stricter v2 requirement; v1\nomitted it. Validate ID/access token types according to their actual contracts,\nnot by decoding arbitrary JWT claims. Rotation/expiry/sleep/revocation fail closed.\nSame-identity token refresh restarts the one-shot-bootstrap engine and restores\ncompatible in-session history; it re-confirms workspace-write permission and\nnever replays remote/tool effects. Declining workspace-write confirmation keeps\nthe draft/cancels Send without restart. Any changed ContextBinding (including\nlocal access/model/configuration/MCP inventory) starts a new conversation; its\nold history is inspect-only under the new binding. MCP-only token rotation also\nrebootstraps/restores an identical binding, but retains working v1's current\nproject-access confirmation without an SSO refresh or another write prompt.\nNative scope additionally binds per-server MCP grant IDs/catalog revisions absent\nfrom engine equality; re-sign-in/sign-out/removal/revision change retires the\nconversation. Expiry alone leaves inference usable with that non-required server\nunavailable and native calls denied. This continuity is distinct from inspect-only\nimported history. No token or refresh credential enters React, diagnostics, process arguments or\ninherited environment. One-time operator-entered inputs remain transient.\n\nUse independent v2 bundle/data/Keychain namespaces. Protected work is encrypted\nand account/context-scoped; no plaintext fallback. Reviewed import creates a new\nv2 data generation, never modifies v1, transfers no credentials/approvals and\nretains a restartable receipt/rollback generation. Historical conversations are\ninspect-only until fresh-context compatibility is qualified.\n\nTests cross native process and identity interfaces: malformed/oversized bootstrap,\nqueue pressure, wrong context, crash/EOF/graceful and forced cleanup, signature/\nissuer/audience/key-rotation rejection, scoped sign-out, protected histories and\nNode-free installed inventory. Native/live/final signed-byte proof remain separate.\nSee [selected design](../architecture/DESIGN.md) for identity/crypto proposals.\n# 4. Managed HTTP enters native configuration authority\n\nDate: 2026-10-16. Status: selected following owner candidate selection; independent G01 verdict\nand implementation readiness are tracked in planning/status.md. Implements D014,D016 and C14/C15 requirements.\n\nV1's workbench has real request-document and transport depth. Its redactions-based\nredirect guard is not explicit tenant-credential classification, Live mode lacks\napplication destination binding, and its post-401 retry can resend a mutation.\nThe stricter v2 review/no-replay policy must be enforced below every Send path.\n\nKeep RequestDocument reconciliation separate. Native execution admission captures\na fresh immutable effective request and explicitly distinguishes tenant-managed,\nworkbench-owned and anonymous credentials. Known managed reads need capability/\ndestination admission. Known mutations need exact Configuration review. Unknown\nmanaged methods/paths are denied, even if presented as a read or generic draft.\nOrdinary workbench-owned/anonymous HTTP remains supported.\n\nReplace the editable SCM collection-ID credential gate with native-owned\ncapability registration. Imported IDs/references cannot authorize tenant secret\nresolution. An edited token_url/request URL is admitted against the explicit\ncredential destination before secrets are obtained; an ID change never creates\na capability. Preserve operator-reviewed endpoint configuration separately.\n\nBind review to expanded URL/method/headers/body and file hashes, variables,\ncookies, credential/context/destination and resource classification. Recheck\nbefore dispatch; changed input/context/files require fresh review. Rust constructs\nthe actual request; renderer fields cannot mark it preapproved.\n\nManaged redirects are disabled initially: a 3xx is shown, and following it is a\nnew prepared/admitted request. Generic redirects preserve bounded cookie/credential\norigin policy. No automatic workbench resend follows HTTP401, refresh, reconnect,\nlost response, import or history replay. Refresh can precede a new explicit Send.\n\nThe built-in catalogue has 193 request specifications, 81 with mutation methods;\nthis is metadata parity, not a claim of dedicated/live-qualified product support.\nTests cover imported/edited requests, unknown managed endpoints, direct IPC bypass,\nambiguous paths/auth headers, same/cross-origin redirects, stale cookie/file/\nvariable/context bindings, double Apply and post-401/lost-response no resend.\n\nThis is a stricter v2 product contract, not an allegation that v1 failed its own\ncontract. See [selected design](../architecture/DESIGN.md).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/planning/IMPLEMENTATION-PLAN.md docs/planning/dependencies.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/planning/IMPLEMENTATION-PLAN.md docs/planning/dependencies.md'","aggregated_output":"# Prisma AIRS Desktop v2 implementation plan\n\nStatus: product interview decisions D001–D023 accepted; D024/D025 settle source\nterms and all four architecture candidates; D026 makes Codex the independent\nchunk and phase reviewer. G00/G01 passed; implementation proceeds from C01. A passing plan review is not a passing architecture,\nSDK, application, live-tenant, or package review.\n\n## Outcome and exclusions\n\nDeliver a fresh macOS Apple Silicon desktop application with React presentation\nin Tauri, a focused Rust application core, direct Rust SCM SDK integration, and\nthe native Rust Harness engine. Academy supplies shared presentation and remains\nthe authority for remote learning. Preserve agreed working v1 behavior, using\nv1 as requirements reference. Do not translate its GPUI implementation wholesale.\n\nNo desktop billing, media-generation backend, local MySQL/Redis server stack,\nNode/Bun backend runtime, TypeScript workers, speculative browser client, or\nin-app updater is included. Academy gamification remains inside Academy. Gateway\nusage/cost/budget/limits remain operational product functionality. Build-time\nJavaScript tooling and React WebView JavaScript are expected.\n\nSource destination: `ssh://git@git-ssh.cdot.io/cdot/prisma-airs-desktop-v2.git`.\nCheckout: `/Users/cdot/development/redtail/prisma-airs-desktop-v2`.\n\n## Architectural guiding principles\n\n1. Design deep modules: substantial behavior behind a small domain interface.\n   Apply the deletion test; do not wrap every SDK method in a pass-through module.\n2. The interface is the test surface. Exercise production transitions and\n   adapters; use independent wire/protocol expectations for external contracts.\n3. One adapter is a hypothetical seam; two real adapters justify a seam. Shared\n   Academy presentation has real web/desktop consumers. Do not build speculative\n   browser/backend/plugin architectures for this desktop delivery.\n4. Rust owns authority and locality for tenant/credential/workspace context,\n   request lifecycle, configuration review, protected storage, and approvals.\n   React owns presentation and editable input, not permission to execute.\n5. The SDK owns its declared transport/authentication contracts. The application\n   owns interactive identity, OS storage, context admission, and workflow policy.\n   Do not duplicate SDK retry/pagination/OAuth policy without a documented need.\n6. Unknown outcomes and partial/stale/denied/unsupported data remain distinct.\n   Never map an unknown verdict to allow or a cancelled write to successful rollback.\n7. Host credentials and capabilities are explicit. No ambient environment-file\n   discovery, inherited production fallback, generic renderer secret access,\n   unrestricted native plugin grants, or cross-origin credential forwarding.\n8. Shared appearance does not imply shared learner state. Tenant operations,\n   Academy identity/progression, Harness inference identity, and MCP grants stay\n   separately scoped. A tenant switch does not sign the learner out of Academy.\n9. Keep provenance and reproducibility local to dependency selection. Exact\n   versions, source hashes, build inputs, notices, and protocol pins are evidence;\n   a neighboring active worktree is not a release dependency.\n10. Measure native usability/performance and the actual installed package. A\n    successful browser render or mocked IPC assertion is insufficient evidence\n    for Tauri host behavior, VoiceOver, Keychain, sidecar isolation, or notarization.\n\nConcrete interfaces, crate decomposition, shared package extraction, and storage\nformats are outputs of G01. This plan defines behavior and testable work, not\nunreviewed Rust signatures or a predetermined hierarchy of tiny modules.\n\n## Ordered phases and chunks\n\nEvery Cxx has its own instruction file under [chunks](chunks/README.md). Complete\nchunks in numeric order. A dependency delay blocks its chunk; report it honestly\nand continue only research/preparation explicitly within that chunk. Do not\nsilently advance a later phase or replace a real dependency with a fake to pass.\nFor a prolonged upstream stall, report the exact dependency and offer an owner\nreorder/scope decision. Do not wait indefinitely or reorder silently; an approved\nchange updates predecessors, plan and review evidence before execution.\n\n| Phase | Chunks   | Result                                                           |\n|-------|----------|------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| P00   | G00, G01 | Confirmed plan; selected and reviewed architecture               |\n| P01   | C01–C03  | Shared desktop shell, native core, first complete Runtime scan   |\n| P02   | C04–C07  | Academy identity, learner presentation, assessment, offline work |\n| P03   | C08–C12  | Runtime/DLP administration, Sessions, reports, Library           |\n| P04   | C13–C15  | Existing Red Team/Model reads and complete API Client workflows  |\n| P05   | C16–C18  | Gateway workspace/configuration, reviewed changes, inference     |\n| P06   | C19–C22  | Native Harness, Rust sign-in, typed host tools, workspace MCP    |\n| P07   | C23–C24  | Reviewed imports, native quality, accessibility/privacy closure  |\n| P08   | C25–C26  | Controlled live qualification; signed installed DMG acceptance   |\n| P09   | C27–C44  | UI v2 \"Refined Native\": spec, shell, wizard, destinations (D058); re-cut from C35 as one screen per chunk ported from the prototype (D059) |\n\n### G00 — Plan confirmation\n\nFinish the decision ledger, baseline coverage, chunk instructions, review/test\nprotocols, dependency gates, and architecture handoff. Obtain an actual independent\nplan review meeting D019. Product decisions are accepted and the owner explicitly\nselected the architecture candidates and source terms in D024/D025. Continue the\nrequested design work; no further blanket scope question is needed. Architecture\nreview and implementation evidence remain distinct gates.\n\n### G01 — Architecture improvement workflow\n\nFollow [the handoff](architecture-handoff.md) and the requested\nimprove-codebase-architecture skill. Explore the named areas, show a temporary\nHTML report with before/after candidates, ask the owner to choose, and grill the\nselected design. Produce ADRs, interface/test ownership, dependency/import\ndecisions, and concrete chunk command specifications. Review the architecture\nat least 9/10 in every critical dimension and overall. Revise this plan if the\nselected design affects it, then re-review affected planning evidence.\n\nNo application scaffolding, shared-source refactoring, real-data migration,\nproduct endpoint calls, or packaging implementation begins before G01 passes.\n\n## Per-chunk execution contract\n\n1. Read AGENTS/CLAUDE, glossary, accepted ADRs, the chunk, predecessor receipts,\n   dependency manifest, test catalogue, and its canonical implementer prompt.\n2. Confirm a clean approved source snapshot and identify unrelated user work.\n   Resolve dependency availability/provenance and baseline check limitations.\n3. Implement only that chunk and approved supporting changes. A shared Academy,\n   SDK, or Harness change needs its own reviewed revision and regression evidence;\n   preserve active work and do not move another project's main branch casually.\n4. Run exact checks from the approved command manifest. Use deterministic local\n   fixtures and literal external contracts, then the appropriate native tests.\n   Never call hosted application endpoints by accident.\n5. Record commands, environments, inputs, results, failures, hashes, limitations,\n   and rollback rehearsal. Save raw sensitive evidence privately; commit redacted\n   summaries with references/hashes, not credentials or customer bodies.\n6. Freeze source and dependency inputs. Request actual independent Codex\n   review through [the independent prompt](prompts/review-chunk.md) (D026).\n7. If any critical dimension/overall score is below 9, required fixes remain,\n   source changed, or mandatory evidence is absent: remediate, rerun affected\n   checks, freeze a new snapshot, and request a new review of the same chunk.\n8. Complete only after the receipt and evidence validate against current inputs.\n   After a phase's last chunk, obtain the cumulative Codex phase review\n   ([prompt](prompts/review-phase.md)) before starting the next phase.\n   Make a focused commit; revalidate that its tree matches the reviewed tree.\n   Later edits invalidate affected receipts and trigger targeted regression\n   checks/re-review. Preserve failed attempts; never pressure a reviewer to pass.\n\n## Command contract to establish in C01\n\nThese commands are a required future interface, not tools that exist today:\n\n```text\ncargo run --locked -p xtask -- check --chunk Cxx --manifest docs/architecture/checks.json\ncargo run --locked -p xtask -- test --chunk Cxx --manifest docs/architecture/checks.json\ncargo run --locked -p xtask -- native --chunk Cxx --manifest docs/architecture/checks.json\ncargo run --locked -p xtask -- review --chunk Cxx --reviewer codex --manifest docs/architecture/checks.json\ncargo run --locked -p xtask -- review --phase Pxx --reviewer codex --manifest docs/architecture/checks.json\ncargo run --locked -p xtask -- complete --chunk Cxx --manifest docs/architecture/checks.json\ncargo run --locked -p xtask -- package --kind preview --manifest docs/architecture/checks.json\ncargo run --locked -p xtask -- package --kind release-candidate --manifest docs/architecture/checks.json\n```\n\nC01 creates the controller and a versioned manifest of exact commands, working\ndirectories, targets, features, fixtures, timeouts, and expected evidence. It\ncannot implement the later chunks itself. G01 specifies actual Rust/pnpm/Tauri\ncommands after choosing the workspace; a future engineer must not guess them or\nclaim these placeholder commands already ran. Document/test the controller's\nrejection of missing, stale, malformed, edited, or sub-threshold receipts.\n\n## Coverage and evidence\n\n- [Feature baseline](feature-baseline.md): existing working/preview/stub behavior.\n- [Parity matrix](parity-matrix.md): work ownership and release closure.\n- [Dependency gates](dependencies.md): accepted inputs and unresolved qualification.\n- [Test catalogue](test-catalogue.md): named behavioral/native/artifact test cases.\n- [Review protocol](review-protocol.md): scoring, receipt fields, invalidation.\n- [Release contract](release-contract.md): live scope, signing, package/install proof.\n- [Mandatory live checks](live-checks.md): fixed L-family/F-smoke requirements and\n  owner-approved per-workflow live scope; no access-driven silent reductions.\n- [Distribution gate](distribution.md): owner-selected source/binary terms,\n  Academy permission, framework disposition and exact shipped notices.\n- [Status](status.md): authoritative current state; planned is not implemented.\n\nG01 must convert each chunk's named tests into exact invocation commands and\nobservable assertions. Tests cannot simply repeat implementation decisions.\nFresh signed-artifact checks bind the final DMG hash and installed bytes; source\nor fixture checks do not substitute for them.\n\n## Expected sequencing dependencies\n\nThe scan proof can use accepted SDK Phase 3 after native macOS qualification.\nManagement/DLP uses accepted Phase 4 or a later explicitly reviewed compatible\npin. Existing Red Team/Model reads need verified Phase 5 capabilities; complete\nGateway needs Phase 6 capabilities. Harness/Academy transports are application\nwork, not delivered by the SCM SDK. Keep these upstream dependencies visible and\ndo not require unrelated SDK operations merely because they share a library.\n\nFirst-slice metrics establish named budgets before C04. Every UI chunk includes\nkeyboard/focus checks. Later introducing chunks register their numeric workload\nbudgets before implementation/acceptance measurement under T20 governance; budget\nchanges need recorded rationale and owner or independent reviewer acknowledgement.\nC24/C26 compare these pre-registered values, not newly fitted limits. C24 closes\nthe full native/VoiceOver/performance and cross-feature offline matrix.\nPreview packages may demonstrate accepted scope but must name missing features.\nOnly C26 can establish stable installed-package readiness.\n\n## Completion and delivery\n\nStable v2 requires every agreed working-v1 parity row closed, all chunk/phase\nreviews passing, designated live acceptance complete, no missing mandatory\nchecks, and C26's installed signed/notarized DMG evidence. V1 stays available;\nfresh credentials and reviewed imports remain explicit. Updates initially use\nmanual signed DMGs with rollback instructions.\n\nThe active implementation task may deliver reviewed Git commits to the exact\nowner-specified remote using normal pushes. Application release publication,\nhosting changes, and promotion over v1 must remain within the owner's explicit\nrelease instruction; preparing a signed package does not by itself publish it.\n# Dependency and source gates\n\nArchitecture proposed exact first-slice versions in docs/architecture/qualification.md.\nAs of C01 (2026-10-17) the desktop graph is resolved, locked and compiled on\nthe macOS ARM64 host; the resolved inputs are recorded below. Later SDK\nfamilies and other inputs remain gated at their consuming chunks.\n\n## Source ownership\n\n| Input          | Inspected revision                       | Ownership/use                                           |\n|----------------|------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------|\n| cdot-framework | e3553b27a3d6bf457a2e585c5ae479c7db89a17b | Selective patterns/modules after provenance evaluation  |\n| Desktop v1     | 74754c02b19d87187b6dca81f64550a669b0f841 | Behavioral/contracts reference; preserve checkout       |\n| Academy        | 7f3a28c4082a5bb278bbff5d79fab106c240155a | Shared React source; hosted learning authority          |\n| Native Harness | 9331a5839bc6a763d94dee7584746f58bbbe1181 | Desktop-capable native engine/protocol input            |\n| SDK Phase 3    | ec6230d2a19b9c835c9e4c9487795b38ad538af3 | Accepted scan subset; native/live qualification pending |\n| scm-sdk crate  | crates.io 0.1.0, sha256 6d555d955c3734dd5ee78f7173569bdecd4426095b2ffedc4b77d8f0f2c2e8d9, from 4f65dc48686a3b701ed0585812fcca46859f7e5a | D027 scan input from C02 (scan feature only); 164 package unit tests pass natively; live qualification is C25 |\n| SDK Phase 4    | 32cbcb6d9b04478aeca21e767ed6ddb8949ec7bb | Accepted management/DLP/IAM subset; qualify exact uses  |\n\nDo not consume an active neighboring worktree via an unpinned path and later\nclaim the accepted commit was built. Obtain a clean revision-specific source\nsnapshot/package without secrets or ignored local configuration. Manifest both\nits origin revision and actual source/package hash. A dependency update reopens\nthe affected contract/native/artifact checks and review. Never fabricate SDK\nmethod parity from a similar TypeScript method name.\n\n## C01 resolved inputs\n\nRecorded by C01 and bound to its review. Locks: `Cargo.lock`, `pnpm-lock.yaml`,\n`rust-toolchain.toml`, `docs/architecture/toolchain.json`,\n`docs/architecture/inputs.lock.json`; the controller's dependency fingerprint\nhashes them with the consumed `.inputs/` bytes and tool identities.\n\n| Input | Resolved | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| Rust toolchain | 1.99.0 (b940084d7 2026-09-28) | Corrected from 1.90.0; proc-macro dylib dyld failure at deployment target 13.0 on macOS 27 (evidence/C01/toolchain-correction.json). Release `strip` removed for the same failure. |\n| Tauri / tauri-build / Tauri CLI / @tauri-apps/api | 2.12.4 / 2.7.3 / 2.12.3 / 2.12.4 | `custom-protocol` added by the Tauri CLI for bundles |\n| ts-rs | 12.0.1 (serde-compat, no-serde-warnings) | Generated declarations committed; `xtask bindings --check` |\n| React / ReactDOM | 19.2.3 | Single instance per consumer (pnpm strict peers) |\n| Tailwind / Vite / Vitest / plugin-react / TypeScript | 4.3.2 / 7.3.6 / 3.2.6 / 5.2.0 / 5.9.3 | Matched to Academy's resolved lock instead of the earlier 4.1.18 / 7.3.0 proposal |\n| Build Node / pnpm | 24.21.0 / 10.6.5 (corepack shim in `.tools/bin`) | Build-only; never shipped |\n| Academy shared presentation | `ai-security-academy` 891f65eef76b53eb87155d0f0f5622228e217a2d on branch `v2/desktop-presentation` (base 7f3a28c4) | Pushed to the Academy origin on 2026-10-17 (owner-approved; main untouched). `xtask fetch-inputs --source <academy clone>` materializes `.inputs/academy` (tree sha256 1c4ce702…); a fresh clone from origin reproduces it (evidence/C01/clean-checkout). |\n\nThird-party notices for the shipped graph are in `docs/architecture/notice-inventory.json`\n(`xtask notices --check`). The Academy package is Academy-owned and recorded by\nits pinned commit, not relicensed.\n\n## Required gates\n\n- **Distribution terms:** G01 records owner-approved v2 source/binary terms,\n  Academy redistribution permission and every framework-derived import's\n  disposition under [distribution.md](distribution.md). No extraction before\n  clearance; C26 verifies notices/terms against exact shipped inventory.\n\n- **Provenance:** evaluate each direct import's actual license/notice and allowed\n  usage. cdot-framework is not blanket MIT and its billing-retention condition\n  cannot be erased by renaming. Prefer independently implemented patterns where\n  direct copying is unsuitable. Academy sharing is owner-directed; establish\n  package ownership and third-party notices rather than inventing a license.\n- **Academy packaging:** React19/Tailwind4/Vite7 are observed in Academy, while\n  the framework uses React18/Tailwind3. Select a compatible pinned desktop stack\n  and one shared presentation source. The existing UI package re-exports app\n  source and is not a ready independently reusable full learner app.\n- **Shared-source changes:** propose the smallest extraction that supports actual\n  web/desktop consumers. Keep Academy web behavior and hosted contracts stable;\n  run both projects' affected tests. Pin the reviewed shared revision/package\n  before completing its consuming desktop chunk.\n- **SDK scan:** ec6230d2's accepted subset supplies synchronous scan. It requires\n  explicit scan credentials; management OAuth is separate. Accepted Linux\n  reports do not establish macOS ARM64 support. Qualify the native compiler/TLS\n  dependencies, runtime integration, scanner contracts, cancellation, sanitized\n  errors, and unknown outcomes in C02–C03.\n- **SDK administration:** 32cbcb6d's accepted Phase4 is a candidate input for\n  C08–C12. Map every actual operation, option, pagination bound, and error to\n  accepted source/tests. SDK family labels are insufficient proof of parity.\n- **SDK later families:** Phase5 Red Team/Model and Phase6 Gateway were developing\n  or pending during inspection. Their exact accepted pins are prerequisites to\n  C13/C16–C18. Do not consume unfinished work or substitute mocks as completion.\n  Stable v2 must qualify the SDK subset it actually uses, including native/live\n  evidence; it need not claim every unrelated SDK method is complete.\n- **Harness:** retain the desktop-capable native engine/protocol after source and\n  package checks. Do not substitute an older canonical checkout or npm launcher.\n  Rebuild/reseal v2 resources to omit Node/managed CLI where proven unnecessary;\n  old v1 resource hashes do not attest the changed v2 bundle. Preserve inference\n  and MCP routing/context/tool restrictions.\n- **Native host:** select pinned Tauri2/Rust/toolchain dependencies compatible\n  with macOS ARM64 and the chosen SDK. Resolve one application-owned async\n  runtime strategy; do not create an independent runtime per request.\n- **Identity:** specify independent v2 bundle/display/data/Keychain identities in\n  G01. Test v1/v2 coexistence, reset, uninstall, import, and rollback. Do not\n  silently reuse a v1 namespace or change the learner's remote account.\n- **Live environment:** identify designated test tenants/accounts, scopes,\n  reversible resources, budgets, and cleanup/readback before C25 execution.\n  Prod configuration discovery is not mutation authorization. Missing access is\n  a real dependency gap; never report skipped tests as passed.\n- **Hosted OAuth registrations:** G01 maps reuse/new-client/redirect/scope needs\n  for Academy device authorization, Harness company SSO and MCP. Responsible\n  Academy/Gateway/IdP operators own authorized staging/configuration changes and\n  deployment. V2 identity alone neither proves a new registration is required\n  nor authorizes production changes. Resolve before the consuming live tests.\n- **Signing:** identify an authorized Developer ID, provisioning/entitlement\n  requirements, notarization access, and a native acceptance host. Access secrets\n  only at use time through approved storage, never committed plan variables.\n\n## Official host references\n\nTauri supports Rust commands and async messages between presentation and host;\nthe selected command/module interface still requires G01 design and review.\n[Calling Rust](https://v2.tauri.app/develop/calling-rust/)\n\nWindow/WebView capability grants require deliberate scoping; renderer grants do\nnot replace Rust's application authority checks.\n[Capabilities](https://v2.tauri.app/security/capabilities/)\n\nDMG distribution and macOS signing are separate from successful compilation.\n[DMG packaging](https://v2.tauri.app/distribute/dmg/),\n[macOS signing/notarization](https://v2.tauri.app/distribute/sign/macos/).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'D0(14|26|34|5[89]|60)|C31|V2-[RT](03|06|07|08|10|11|19|20|21)|Administration|rotation|Key rotation' docs/planning/decisions.md docs/planning/parity-matrix.md docs/planning/test-catalogue.md docs/planning/status.md docs/planning/WORF-START-HERE.md docs/architecture/ui-v2/screens/boards.md docs/architecture/ui-v2/state-ownership.md docs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'D0(14|26|34|5[89]|60)|C31|V2-[RT](03|06|07|08|10|11|19|20|21)|Administration|rotation|Key rotation' docs/planning/decisions.md docs/planning/parity-matrix.md docs/planning/test-catalogue.md docs/planning/status.md docs/planning/WORF-START-HERE.md docs/architecture/ui-v2/screens/boards.md docs/architecture/ui-v2/state-ownership.md docs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md\"","aggregated_output":"docs/architecture/ui-v2/screens/boards.md:31:| R2Admin | R2App | start=admin | `fxAdmin` (v2/admin/administration.test.tsx) | V2-T19, T20, per-tab labels | C31 |\ndocs/architecture/ui-v2/screens/boards.md:32:| R2Review | R2App | start=admin, overlay=review | `fxAdmin` + `review()`/`rotation()` (same file) | V2-T03, T06, T08; T07 in `ui_v2_reviews` | C31 |\ndocs/planning/WORF-START-HERE.md:82:- **C29 attempt-07: 9/9/9/8/8**, no blockers, accepted under D034 with R1/R2/E1/E2.\ndocs/planning/WORF-START-HERE.md:91:- C30/C31/C32 still need their predecessor-dependent re-reviews after C29 reaches\ndocs/planning/WORF-START-HERE.md:107:executable. C44's cumulative driver runs last. Historical C24-N06 and C31-N01/N04\ndocs/planning/WORF-START-HERE.md:113:C31 and C32 in order. Report every actual score and apply D034's 8-then-9 rule.\ndocs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:14:| P1 | Wizard tenant step draws the scan key, management client secret and Gateway workspace key as text fields (`R2Onboard` `f.scanKey`, `f.secret`, `f.gwKey`) | \"Add scan key…\" button opens the host's native secret sheet; the step then shows a masked indicator and the vault record id | The web layer never holds a secret (D014, C09 reveal ownership). Visual delta: button plus masked state instead of a field |\ndocs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:16:| P3 | State lives in one component with `byTenant` (`R2App` `TS/ts/tPatch`) | Core-owned scopes per [state-ownership.md](state-ownership.md) | D014 authority; prototype state model is normative for invariants only |\ndocs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:18:| P5 | Sidebar lists Home, Inbox, Harness, Academy, Test Cases, API Client, Administration | Same, backed by Rust feature ids `home`, `inbox`, `harness`, `academy`, `library`, `apiClient`, `administration`; `gateway` and `redTeam` move inside Administration; `sessions`, `import`, `settings` reachable from the palette and Administration | One Rust inventory feeds menu, sidebar and palette |\ndocs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:23:| P10 | Administration creation label is a per-product single value | Per-tab creation capability and label in `AdminInventory` | Round 16 finding: wrong label on API keys, Security profiles and Logs tabs |\ndocs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:33:| P20 | Key rotation issues a new key with a 24-hour grace period, a later revocation and a secret recoverable from the Keychain (`REVIEWS.key`, `keyRotated/keySecretClosed/keyDeployed/keyDoneAt`) | Two admitted operations in sequence: `ApiKeys · Version` (additional key, one-time reveal through the existing `RevealSecret` host sheet, never persisted) → local deployment acknowledgement → `ApiKeys · Delete` of the old key with readback. Two distinct timestamps; a lost secret means \"issue another key\" | `changes.rs` 842–846 and `management.rs` 980–988 define Rotate (regenerate, no grace) and Version (additional key); DESIGN.md \"Secrets\" forbids persisting issued keys |\ndocs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:36:| P23 | One rotation flow for every key | Staged Version → Delete rotation for Runtime API keys only; Gateway keys use `Rotate` (regenerate in place) | `gateway_changes::allowed` admits Rotate, not Version, for Gateway keys |\ndocs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:37:| P24 | Minimum window 1180×720 (DESIGN-V2 §4, tokens.md) | The host keeps `min_inner_size(900, 600)` until C34 because C01-N09 (re-run cumulatively by C24-N06) pins the legacy minimum; the v2 shell is laid out to stay usable without horizontal scroll at 900×600 (rail collapsed to icons below 1280 CSS px) and C28-N07 asserts it at that size. C34 raises the minimum together with the legacy-script retirement | Legacy native acceptance must keep passing while both shells exist (D058 §5) |\ndocs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:38:| P25 | Revision of P1/P2: \"secrets enter only through a host secret sheet\" | The retained, reviewed entry path (C02/C09) is a webview password field read through a ref, cleared immediately and sent once inside the intent as `EnteredSecret` (zeroized, never in React state, never logged); the host owns the *reveal* sheet (`RevealSecret`) and the clipboard. C29 uses exactly this path for the scan key, management secret and Gateway key; no new host entry sheet is built | The C27 spec assumed an entry sheet the host does not have; the reviewed pattern already satisfies D014 and the C24 canary checks (no secret in any AX tree or file) |\ndocs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:45:| P32 | First-run import returns to the wizard | A v1 import during first run commits its tenants through the reviewed C23 flow; the desktop then has a tenant, so the first-run draft ends (its credentials are cleaned up) and the shell opens on the imported tenant; its scan key is added in Administration (the reviewed C08/C09 credential setup) | A tenant that exists is never sent back through a first-run wizard (P30), and the wizard cannot wrap an existing tenant without duplicating it |\ndocs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:47:| P34 | Inbox items with `kind`, `severity` and `explanation` per item | `InboxEntry` carries the overview item (Runtime, Gateway) or the C29 setup warning with its hold (listed, with every action, whatever the read status: a warning exists before any read); the explanation is per kind in the presentation's copy, not invented per item; `deadline` is absent (P20). Its action carries an `InboxTarget` (destination, product, Administration inventory, item name): a key item opens Administration on the API keys inventory filtered to the key, a setup warning opens where it is fixed (Settings for the scan key or management client, AI Gateway, AI Red Teaming) | The admitted reads report no per-item explanation or deadline; the target was added after the attempt-01 review found actions landing on the destination's default view |\ndocs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:52:| P39 | `AdminInventory` generated from screens/administration-inventory.md | Implemented as a Rust table (`admin_inventory::inventory`) carried on `ShellSnapshot.administration` in the v2 shell only (null in the legacy shell, whose literal snapshot and leak canary are unchanged; the presentation's fixture copy is pinned to the table by a core test); a unit test and the `ui_v2_reviews` suite pin every offered (kind, action) to `changes::is_allowed` and every denied pair to its refusal, so a label can never outrun the core. Read and Change tabs render the retained views (the same reads and reviewed changes) embedded under the accordion with the inventory's labels; Unavailable tabs and products show their gate and nothing else; Removed tabs are not rendered. The Gateway MCP servers tab lists the chosen workspace's catalog through `listMcpServers` | One source of truth for dispositions, checked against the matrices the core enforces (V2-T20, V2-R11) |\ndocs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:54:| P41 | Rotation state per tenant with two stored timestamps | `Tenant.rotations` (persisted, never a secret): driven from settled `ApiKeys` results of the active context (a Version read back starts one; an unknown Version starts an unconfirmed one; a failed one records nothing; the old key's Delete read back absent completes it; a rejected Delete keeps it with the reason; an unknown one is reconciled by a complete key inventory read), plus `acknowledgeDeployment{application}` stored once. The reminder is the Inbox item `rotation-<application>` while a key is confirmed and the old one not read back gone. The one-time secret stays on its change result for the context it was applied in: Reveal is offered only while the core holds it; any sheet exit forgets it and a context change drops it | The issued secret must never be persisted (I6) while the stage and timestamps must survive reads, switches and relaunches (I7) |\ndocs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:56:| P43 | C31 live row runs through `xtask live-run` | `live-run --family L03 --subject C31` runs the C31 topic routine inside L03's designation and writes its receipts and run record under evidence/C31/live, leaving C25's receipts untouched (the consumption ledger stays C25's single ledger) | The live row needs the reviewed runner and the owner's designation, not a second harness |\ndocs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:58:| P45 | The mcp and keys follow-ups open a guardrail review and the key rotation flow | `proposeChange` admits, besides the Runtime and DLP kinds, an API key `version` (the hand-off of a rotation: it opens the C31 review sheet and its staged sequence) and a guardrail of the conversation's own Gateway workspace (create or update only), staged with that workspace's reference so its review is bound to it; Gateway deletes and every other Gateway change, and a key rotation in place, stay refused. The follow-up of a key hand-off shows the rotation stage from `ShellSnapshot.rotations`. The mcp lifecycle's guardrail is described as what it is (a pii-check guardrail on all messages while the unlisted server is reviewed); no guardrail expresses an MCP allow list | A proposal only ever opens the operator's own review (D014); the follow-ups must be core-owned, not page-invented |\ndocs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:62:| P49 | The core decides from the tool's write flag whether the native confirmation is required (C21 assumed) | No such branch existed: every decision was in-page. The core now classifies each decision (`HarnessApproval.effect`): a desktop read, or a command while the project is read-only, is `readOnly`; a file change, a command with changes allowed and any workspace MCP tool (whose effect the desktop cannot know; tools carry no trusted annotations) is `mayChange`, and allowing it opens the native confirmation (`Effect::ConfirmHarness`), whose private completion alone sends the allow; refused, the decision stays pending; a stale one is dismissed. In TypeScript `effect` is optional only so pre-C32 snapshots type-check, and absent is treated as `mayChange` | In-page consent must never authorise a change (D058, C32) |\ndocs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:69:| P56 | \"Open in Playground\" sets the Playground's prompt, direction and profile | It hands the case's profile, prompt, response and application to Administration's Runtime Playground (the v2 home of the Runtime scan form) and opens that tab; the operator runs the scan there, and that scan names the case. The scan form has no separate direction: a case's prompt and response are its directions. Nothing is scanned by opening it, and Inspect dispatches nothing | The Runtime scan form (C03) is the admitted execution surface; direction is expressed by which fields hold text |\ndocs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:73:attempt-01 review (R3–R6); P22–P23 after attempt-03 (F3a, F3b); P24–P25 were recorded in C28 and P26–P32 in C29 and P33–P38 in C30 and P39–P43 in C31, P44–P51 in C32 and P52–P56 in C33 when implementation met the host as it is (P26–P28 revised after the C29 attempt-01 review; P26–P27 again, and P32 added, after attempt-02; P33–P35 revised and P36–P38 added after the C30 attempt-01 review).\ndocs/planning/test-catalogue.md:80:  issuer/client/audience/time/signature/JWKS/algorithm/key-rotation validation;\ndocs/architecture/ui-v2/state-ownership.md:55:| `s.product`, `s.exp`, `s.ptab`, `s.allOpen`, `s.adminNav`, `s.adminQ`, `s.tree` | Administration product, expanded set, tab per product, expand-all, nav mode, search, tree | UI | admin store | transient | kept |\ndocs/architecture/